- @next: Continue...

@next

Early estimates put the death toll between 7 and 25 and displaced at least 35 thousand, mostly Indian Tamils. 

The CWC called for some kind of response from its ally in the UNP over these incidents. President JR Jayawardene seemingly agreed with the CWC saying "What is the example we as leaders of the governing party are setting to our followers and the rest of our countrymen? ... I must have reason to be proud of the party of which I am leader. If I cannot it is better for me to retire ... and let those who believe that the harming of innocent people and property that has happened recently is the way to solve the problem that faces this multi-racial, multi-religious and multi-caste society takeover the leadership of the party".

Many including in the CWC, TULF and ACTC blame the recent attacks on the statements made last week by members of the UNP in parliament. The MP from Ratnapura was expelled and replaced and a cabinet member went abroad for some time for their roles in instigating the violence. The MP's involved with the racial rhetoric in Early August were quick to expunge their statements.

A high-level meeting was held between the TULF, UNP and CWC to cool down tensions. The TULF reiterated and confirmed their non-violent stance to greater autonomy. The UNP also made moves to include more Tamils in local police. Despite negotiations, the Sinhalese view this as capitulation towards seperatists and Tamils are enraged from a lack of investigation by police.

- @and: The militants?

@and
on-arrival:{!
    jaffna_d_control -= 3;
    batticaloa_d_control -= 1;
    trincomalee_d_control -= 1;
    vavuniya_d_control -= 2;
    mullaitivu_d_control -= 2;
    mannar_d_control -= 2;
    kilinochchi_d_control -= 2;
!}

The militants, chiefly the PLOTE and LTTE gained further support for their violent cause. The TULF's passivity to the riots and their participation in the DDC election's are the main reasons for the change in attitude. In addition the militants have further built up their presence, building safe houses in the cities, and camps deep in the jungles. 

At least for now the violence has seemingly come to an end. But the UNP's support among Tamils has reduced, the militants have gained strenght and nationalist fervour is rising. In response a state of emergency has been declared briefly in case violence flares up again, the SLFP needs to watch out.